      "text": "Some people have uninsured motorist coverage umbrellas with millions and millions of dollars, which is great if you could afford it. I highly recommend getting as much uninsured/underinsured motorist coverage as you can afford. But then the other scenario where this, where this coverage comes into play is, let's say the person who causes the crash has bodily injury insurance, but not enough to cover your damages. Sometimes people only have $10,000, which might seem like a lot of money, but not if you have a big hospital bill and you're out of work and things like that. Sometimes they only have $25,000. So what if the at-fault party has even $50,000 of coverage, right? But your damages are worth three, four, five, six hundred thousand, right? You're leaving all that money on the table unless if you have uninsured/underinsured coverage, boom. Now your insurance company steps in. So the difference between where the defendant's bodily injury coverage ends, that's where they basically step in. So if the defendant had a $10,000 policy, all your damages above ten thousand are onto your un-uninsured/underinsured motorist coverage, and then you can collect up to those policy limits, and then sometimes you can even collect more than the policy limits. So that's why these coverages are so important. And I know insurance is one of those things that nobody wants to need, right? Nobody wants to... you don't really wanna pay for it 'cause you're hoping you don't need it. But it's you wanna have that umbrella on a rainy day. You know what I'm saying?"
